{
 "schema": "route_profile_crosscheck/1",
 "note": "Shared fixture. The walkable height profile exists twice -- in the offline compiler (Python, terrain_harvest/routes.py) and in the plugin lab (Java, LabGeometry) -- because the compiler authors Routes into a world file without a server and the lab fits paths at runtime against live blocks. Neither can call the other. Two copies of a rule drift, and these two had: they disagreed on 86% of profiles, because Java pinned the path to its anchors and Python did not. Java was right. Python was always walkable only because it was free to ignore the anchor, which is a path that does not reach its door. Python now pins too, the two agree on every profile tested, and infeasibility -- anchors further apart than the columns allow -- is reported rather than absorbed silently into whichever end loses.",
 "rules": {
  "median_window": 7,
  "max_step": 1,
  "passes": 3,
  "anchors": "the first and last columns are PINNED to their raw height and never clamped",
  "faults": "infeasibility is reported as itself -- anchors further apart than the columns allow -- rather than as a step fault at a position the algorithm chose arbitrarily"
 },
